Q: How to implement SQLCipher when using SQLiteOpenHelper A: <p>I also want to use SQLCipher via SQLiteOpenHelper, and i read this pages, but unfortunately, the answers on this page couldn't help me at the time. I also searched on other websites, but all I found were older versions of the tutorials, and I didn't get the results I wanted. Worse still, they confused me more.</p>
<p>Finally, I read the source code and figured out how SQLiteOpenHelper provided by SQLCipher works in newer version. I shared it below and try to help the person who wants to use SQLCipher via SQLiteOpenHelper.</p>
<p>At first,  add dependencies in build.gradle.kts file of you module.</p>
<pre class="lang-kts prettyprint-override"><code>dependencies {
    //......
    implementation(&quot;net.zetetic:sqlcipher-android:4.12.0@aar&quot;)
    implementation(&quot;androidx.sqlite:sqlite:2.2.0&quot;)
}
</code></pre>
<p>And before use sqlcipher/SQLiteOpenHelper, you should load library：</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>System.loadLibrary(&quot;sqlcipher&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>it's better if you wrap above code with try catch.</p>
<p>Like use the original SQLiteOpenHelper, you should define a subclass of the SQLiteOpenHelper class (provided by sqlcipher)</p>
<p>Then comes the important part. Here I'll state the conclusion directly.</p>
<p>you should pass the password via constructor of SQLiteOpenHelper like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>super(context, DB_NAME_NOTE, new String(password)
        , null
        , DATABASE_VERSION
        , DATABASE_MIN_VERSION
        , new DefaultDatabaseErrorHandler()
        , null
        , true);
</code></pre>
<p>BUT!!! At this point, the password will not be checked for correctness. Password check only when you call function getWritableDatabase() or getReadableDatabase() (Both of them call the function getDatabaseLocked()). If the password was wrong, the function will throw a Throwable with a message: the general meaning is that this file is not a database file.(This is only natural. because sqlcipher try to decrypt the database file with wrong password, so it couldn't get any useful infomation.)</p>
<p>So, finally, I implement the code like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>    // my constructor
    private NoteSecureDBHelper(Context context, char[] password) {
        super(context, DB_NAME_NOTE, new String(password)
                , null
                , DATABASE_VERSION
                , DATABASE_MIN_VERSION
                , new DefaultDatabaseErrorHandler()
                , null
                , true);
        Arrays.fill(pw, '0');   // erase password immediately.
    }

    // singleton.
    public static synchronized NoteSecureDBHelper getInstance(Context context, char[] password) {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new NoteSecureDBHelper(context, password);
            try{
                instance.openDbLink();
            }catch (Throwable e){
                instance = null;    // it's necessary to set the instance to null
                throw e;
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>(You can read the source code of SQLiteOpenHelper too, and tips, the member variable mPassword is the key.)</p>

Q: How to configure NSwag.msbuild to work across platform building net.core projects? A: <p>In your csproj file add the following code before the closing tag &lt;/project&gt;</p>
<pre><code>  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;GeneratedClientsRoot&gt;./*/&lt;/GeneratedClientsRoot&gt;
    &lt;GeneratedClientsWildcard&gt;$(GeneratedClientsRoot)*.cs&lt;/GeneratedClientsWildcard&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;GeneratedClients Include=&quot;$(GeneratedClientsWildCard)&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;NSwagFiles Include=&quot;$(GeneratedClientsRoot)nswag.json&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;SwaggerFiles Include=&quot;$(GeneratedClientsRoot)swagger.json&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;Content Include=&quot;@(NSwagFiles);@(SwaggerFiles)&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;MissingClients Include=&quot;@(NSwagFiles-&gt;'%(Directory)')&quot; Exclude=&quot;@(GeneratedClients-&gt;'%(Directory)')&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

  &lt;Target Name=&quot;EnforceBuild&quot; BeforeTargets=&quot;BeforeBuild&quot; Condition=&quot;'@(MissingClients)' != ''&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Message Text=&quot;Missing Clients detected: @(MissingClients)&quot; Importance=&quot;high&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;Exec Command=&quot;$(NSwagExe_Net80) run %(NSwagFiles.RelativeDir)%(NSwagFiles.Filename).json&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
      &lt;NewContent Include=&quot;$(GeneratedClientsWildCard)&quot; Exclude=&quot;@(GeneratedClients)&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;Message Text=&quot;Generated missing clients: @(NewContent-&gt;'%(FileName)')&quot; Importance=&quot;high&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
      &lt;Compile Include=&quot;@(NewContent)&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;/Target&gt;

  &lt;Target Name=&quot;GenerateClients&quot; BeforeTargets=&quot;BeforeBuild&quot; 
          Inputs=&quot;@(NSwagFiles);@(SwaggerFiles);@(GeneratedClients)&quot; 
          Outputs=&quot;$(TargetPath)&quot; 
          Condition=&quot;'@(MissingClients)' == ''&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Message Text=&quot;Generating swagger clients&quot; Importance=&quot;high&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;Exec Command=&quot;$(NSwagExe_Net80) run %(NSwagFiles.RelativeDir)%(NSwagFiles.Filename).json&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/Target&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The first property GeneratedClientsRoot is the folder pattern where your files will exist. If you create a folder for each nswag.json then the default &quot;./*/&quot; is what you need.</p>
<p>If for example you want all clients in the root of your project simply change GeneratedClientsRoot to &quot;./&quot;</p>
<p>The above code will force generate the clients if there are none created and then will detect if changes to nswag files have happened and regenerate clients accordingly.</p>
<p>--Edit - Added nswag.json config example</p>
<p>This is a partial nswag.json config highlighting important configuration bits.</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;defaultVariables&quot;: null,
    &quot;documentGenerator&quot;: {
        &quot;fromDocument&quot;: {
            &quot;json&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
            &quot;url&quot;: &quot;swagger.json&quot;,
            &quot;output&quot;: null
        }
    },
    &quot;codeGenerators&quot;: {
        &quot;openApiToCSharpClient&quot;: {
            &quot;className&quot;: &quot;MyRestServiceClient&quot;,
            &quot;namespace&quot;: &quot;RestServices.MyRestService&quot;,
            &quot;output&quot;: &quot;MyRestServiceClient.cs&quot;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output needs to be in the same folder as the nswag. Ideally classname matches filename, and don't forget the namespace.</p>
<p>-- Edit 2 - Prerequisites</p>
<p>In order to use this you need to add nugget package nswag.msbuild to your project.</p>
<p>Change NSwagExe_Core80 to NSwagExe_Core21 or whatever framework version you are using.
Also change nswag runtime to the correct runtime or remove it as in the example above.</p>
